 <h4>3. Broyden’s method</h4>
 <p>The Broyden’s method is the mulidimensional equivalent to the secant method. It is similar to Newton’s method, but instead of calculating the inverse Jacobian on each iteration, it uses the secant between two consecutive approximations.</p>
 <img alt="Secant.png" src="./Secant.png" style="height:100pt; width:160pt;"/>
 <p>Although its convergence is slower, it avoids the heavy operation on computing and inverting the Jacobian each time. The solution is performed as follows:</p>
 <p>
  Initial guess -
  <span class="eq">
   <var>
    <span class="vec">⃗</span>
    x⁰
   </var>
   =
   <span class="b0">[</span>
   2; 2; 2; 2
   <span class="b0">]</span>
   =
   <b class="b0">[</b>
   2 2 2 2
   <b class="b0">]</b>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>Find the first approximation by using the Jacobian as in Newton-Raphson’s method</p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <var>
    <span class="vec">⃗</span>
    y⁰
   </var>
   =
   <var>f</var>
   (
   <var>
    <span class="vec">⃗</span>
    x⁰
   </var>
   )  =
   <b class="b0">[</b>
   16 1.59 14 -3
   <b class="b0">]</b>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <b>
    <var>J</var>
   </b>
   <sub>inv</sub>
   =
   <b>inverse</b>
   (
   <var>J</var>
   (
   <var>
    <span class="vec">⃗</span>
    x⁰
   </var>
   )  )  =
   <span class="matrix">
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">-0.477</span>
     <span class="td">2.3</span>
